---
argmap-version: 0.3
title: "Semantics audit: the network reference"
description: "What the shipped reference (the map read as its own Bayesian network, D161) does and does not do, one declared group per question, whole-block copies of the toy battery with suffixed ids: a free premise under one line, under a fan, under a chain, a set of rules no reference can satisfy, and a point on an abduction. Each head states the question and the measured answer."
---

# GENERATED by mvp/scripts/build-audit.mjs from examples/toys/ - edit the
# toy or the spec, then regenerate; tests/audit.test.ts compares.

::r1 [R1: a free premise under one line]: One 0.8 line from A to C. A left free reads 0.500 and C 0.700; A authored 0.5 reads the same. Under the network reference a statement is not pulled below even odds for having consequences: a free premise is a fair coin, and the line is applied to it.

  # r1a: one 0.8 line, both statements free
  @a-r1a [A, the antecedent]: free, no authored number
  @c-r1a [C, the conclusion]: free, no authored number
  $e-r1a [if A then C] 0.8 @c-r1a | @a-r1a: the one rule in the map

  # r1b: the control: the premise authored 0.5
  @a-r1b [A, the antecedent] 0.5: authored at even odds
  @c-r1b [C, the conclusion]: free, no authored number
  $e-r1b [if A then C] 0.8 @c-r1b | @a-r1b: the one rule in the map

::r2 [R2: a free premise under a fan]: Free B under one and under two 0.99 consequences: B stays 0.500 while the consequences read 0.747. On a 0.6/0.3 pair under three consequences B reads 0.643, inside its interval, and the consequences 0.818.

  # r2a: free B, one 0.99 consequence
  @b-r2a [The antecedent]: derived, nothing authors it
  @c1-r2a [First consequence]: derived
  $bc1-r2a [B implies C1] 0.99 @c1-r2a | @b-r2a: the one consequence

  # r2b: free B, two 0.99 consequences
  @b-r2b [The antecedent]: derived, nothing authors it
  @c1-r2b [First consequence]: derived
  @c2-r2b [Second consequence]: derived
  $bc1-r2b [B implies C1] 0.99 @c1-r2b | @b-r2b: the first consequence
  $bc2-r2b [B implies C2] 0.99 @c2-r2b | @b-r2b: the second consequence

  # r2c: B on a 0.6/0.3 pair, three 0.99 consequences
  @b-r2c [The antecedent] 0.6/0.3: the residual pair, interval 0.6 to 0.7
  @c1-r2c [First consequence]: derived
  @c2-r2c [Second consequence]: derived
  @c3-r2c [Third consequence]: derived
  $bc1-r2c [B implies C1] 0.99 @c1-r2c | @b-r2c: the first consequence
  $bc2-r2c [B implies C2] 0.99 @c2-r2c | @b-r2c: the second consequence
  $bc3-r2c [B implies C3] 0.99 @c3-r2c | @b-r2c: the third consequence

::r3 [R3: a chain, and the same line twice]: Free B at the head of a chain, B implies C implies D at 0.99 each: 0.500, 0.748, 0.870. The same 0.99 line twice into one C: B 0.500 and C 0.750, a second copy of a line adding nothing to it.

  # r3a: free B, B implies C implies D at 0.99 each
  @b-r3a [B, the head]: free, nothing authors it
  @c-r3a [C, the middle]: derived
  @d-r3a [D, the tail]: derived
  $bc-r3a [B implies C] 0.99 @c-r3a | @b-r3a: the first link
  $cd-r3a [C implies D] 0.99 @d-r3a | @c-r3a: the second link

  # r3b: free B, the same 0.99 line twice into one conclusion
  @b-r3b [B, the antecedent]: free, nothing authors it
  @c-r3b [C, the conclusion]: derived
  $bc1-r3b [B implies C, once] 0.99 @c-r3b | @b-r3b: the line
  $bc2-r3b [B implies C, again] 0.99 @c-r3b | @b-r3b: the same line again, a second switch forcing the same statement

::r4 [R4: rules no reference can satisfy]: A case for C from a free A and a case against C from a free B, both at 0.9: C reads 0.500 and both antecedents fall to 0.366. Worlds where both lines are in force with both antecedents true are dead, and no reference revives them; the residual is the map's, not the solve's.

  # r4: the zero-set residual
  @a-r4 [A]: free, the first antecedent
  @b-r4 [B]: free, the second antecedent
  @c-r4 [C]: contested
  $ac-r4 [a case for C] 0.9 @c-r4 | @a-r4: argues C from A
  $bc-r4 [a case against C] 0.9 ~@c-r4 | @b-r4: argues against C from B

::r5 [R5: a point on an abduction]: A free A under a 0.99 implication whose consequent carries a bare 0.9 point: B reads 0.899 and A rises to 0.600, the point tilting the premise upward through the line. Abduction, and the shape the W25 warning names.

  # r5: a bare point on the consequent of an abductive line
  @a-r5 [A]: free, the antecedent
  @b-r5 [B] 0.9: a bare point on the consequent
  $ab-r5 [A implies B] 0.99 @b-r5 | @a-r5: the implication
